  {
    "episode_id": 76281,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76281.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, it puts it in jeopardy. The president made a decision to forgive up to $10,000 of loans. And in some cases, $20,000 for many working class and middle class kids. I mean, this idea that forgiving loans is against the working class just doesn't make sense because the kids who need loans aren't the kids of the wealthy. They're usually kids of working class and middle class families. And anyone can tell you that there are a lot of working class families who are proud of their kids going into the skilled trades and their other working class families who want to see their kids go to college. And those kids should have the same opportunities as kids in an affluent area. So this is going to hurt because it creates uncertainty there. And the reason the course decision was so wrong is they assert the role of Congress. Well, Congress passed the Heroes Act. It was shortly after 9-11. And we basically said that the president and the Secretary of Education had wide authority in a national emergency to provide relief. Now, the intent of it may have been originally to help Iraq war veterans, Afghanistan war veterans, but we gave sweeping language to the president and the Secretary of Education. If people thought that what Congress did was too broad, fine, change the law. But no one has tried to change the Heroes Act. No one has been able to change it. Under the plain reading of the statute, the president has the other authority. But the Justice Roberts thought, well, he wants to change the reading. And that's what was galling about that opinion. He's playing Congress when he's supposed to be interpreting what Congress does.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
