  {
    "episode_id": 76281,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76281.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, I'd say the caucus is fairly split, probably slightly more pro student loan forgiveness than not. And one of the things I'd say is to talk about who the people are who are getting student loans. And a lot of them are first generation to go to college. A lot of them are the daughters and sons of working class Americans. So I think we need to be telling the stories more about who this is impacting. The other thing is that we need to be making the case that no other country does this. No other country makes you go into debt to get an education. And we should be talking about education, whether it's a four-year degree or vocational education, as something that should be free in America and not force you to go into debt. But you know, Bakari, one of the things I think the party shouldn't do is pit the working class and skill trades against those who are going to college. I mean, the realities we need both. You're not going to have modern ship factories, modern field factories without the PhDs or college graduates, you're not going to have them without the machinists and electricians. And I think the Democratic Party's message is strong as when we say we're going to need everyone on the team, and we've got to respect the steelworker, we've got to respect the PhD, and not buy into the anti-intellectualism on the right. Because I understand why they don't want people to go to college because they're concerned with all this. Oh, they may learn accurate American history and may realize that we had slavery and that we used to not have women have the right to vote. And that's their problem with college, not that the skills it's for.",
